  -- claim: $\binom{n}{k} = \binom{m}{l},\ 2 \le k \le n/2,\ 2 \le l \le m/2,\ k < l \Rightarrow (n,k,m,l) \in \{(78,2,15,5), (78,2,14,6), (16,2,10,3), (21,2,10,4), (56,2,22,3), (120,2,36,3), (153,2,19,5), (221,2,17,8)\}$ or $(n,k,m,l) = (F_{2i+2}F_{2i+3},\ F_{2i}F_{2i+3},\ F_{2i+2}F_{2i+3}-1,\ F_{2i}F_{2i+3}+1)$ for some $i \ge 1$
  -- Open. B. M. M. de Weger, "Equal binomial coefficients: some elementary considerations", J. Number Theory 63 (1997) 373–386,
  -- stated as Conjecture 2.1 of Blokhuis, Brouwer, de Weger, "Binomial collisions and near collisions", Integers 17 (2017) #A64
